For these Reasonsreasons, no Signssigns that can be given, will actually satisfy, Personspersons in such a Casecase: Letlet the Signssigns that are given, be never so good and infallible, and clearly laid down, they will not serve them. It is like giving a Manman Rulesrules, how to distinguish visible Objectsobjects in the dark: Thethe Thingsthings themselves may be very different, and their Differencedifference may be very well and distinctly described to him; yet all is insufficient to enable him to distinguish them, because he is in the dark. And therefore many Personspersons in such a Casecase spend Timetime in a fruitless Labourlaborlabour, in poring on past Experiencesexperiences, and examining themselves by Signssigns they hear laid down from the Pulpitpulpit, or that they read in Booksbooks; when there is other Workwork for them to do, that is much more expected of them; which, while they neglect, all their Selfself-Examinationsexaminations are like to be in vain, if they should spend never so much Timetime in them. The accursed Thingthing is to be destroyed from their Campcamp, and Acban 〈◊〉
to be slain; and 'tilltill this be done they will be in Troubletrouble. 'Tis not God's Designdesign that Menmen should obtain Assuranceassurance in any other Wayway, than by mortifying Corruptioncorruption, and encreasingincreasing in Gracegrace, and obtaining the lively Exercisesexercises of it. And altho'although Selfself-Examinationexamination be a Dutyduty of great Useuse and Importanceimportance, and by no Meansmeans to be neglected;
yet it is not the principal Meansmeans, by which the Saintssaints do get Satisfactionsatisfaction of their good Estateestate. Assurance is not to be obtain'dobtained so much by Selfself-Examinationexamination, as by Actionaction. The Apostleapostle Paul sought Assuranceassurance chiefly this Wayway, even by forgetting the Thingsthings that were behind, and reaching forth unto those Thingsthings that were before, pressing towards the Markmark for the Prizeprize of the high Callingcalling of God in Christ Jesus; if by any Meansmeans he might attain unto the Resurrectionresurrection of the Deaddead. And it was by this Meansmeans chiefly that he obtain'dobtained Assuranceassurance, 1 Cor. 9.: 26. I therefore so run, as not uncertainly. He obtain'dobtained Assuranceassurance of winning the Prizeprize, more by running, than by considering. The Swiftnessswiftness of his Pacepace, did more towards his Assuranceassurance of a Conquestconquest, than the Strictnessstrictness of his Examinationexamination. Giving all Diligencediligence to grow in Gracegrace, by adding to Faithfaith, Vertuevirtue, &c. isIs the Directiondirection that the Apostleapostle Peter gives us, for making our Callingcalling and Electionelection sure, and having an Entranceentrance ministredministered to us abundantly, into Christ's everlasting Kingdomkingdom; signifying to us, that without this, our Eyeseyes will be dim, and we shall be as Menmen in the Darkdark, that cannot plainly see Thingsthings past or to come, either the Forgivenessforgiveness of our Sinssins past, or our heavenly Inheritanceinheritance that is future, and far off, 2 Pet. 1.: 5,—11. The Wayway to know your Godlinessgodliness, is to renew the visible Exercisesexercises of Gracegrace.—Thethe more the visible Exercisesexercises of Gracegrace are renewed, the more certain you will be. The more frequently these Actingsactings are renewed, the more abiding and confirmed your Assuranceassurance will be.—Thethe more Mensmen'smens Gracegrace is multiplied, the more their Peacepeace is multiplied; 2 Pet. 1.: 2. Grace and Peacepeace be multiplied unto you, through the Knowledgeknowledge of God and Jesus Christ our Lord . Stoddard's Wayway to know Sinceritysincerity and Hypocrisyhypocrisy, p. 139, and 142.

There is such a Thingthing, as a Kindkind of Lovelove or Affectionaffection, that a Manman may have towards Personspersons or Thingsthings, which does properly arise from Selfself-love; a preconceived Relationrelation to himself, or some Respectrespect already manifested by another to him, or some Benefitbenefit already received or depended on, is truly the first Foundationfoundation of his Lovelove, and what his Affectionaffection does wholly arise from; and is what preceedesprecedes any Relishrelish of, or Delightdelight in the Naturenature and Qualitiesqualities inherent in the Beingbeing beloved, as beautiful and amiable, Whenwhen the first Thingthing that draws a Man'sman's Benevolencebenevolence to another, is the beholding those Qualificationsqualifications and Propertiesproperties in him, which appear to him lovely in themselves; and the Subjectsubject of them, on this Accountaccount, worthy of Esteemesteem and Goodwillgoodwill, Lovelove arises in a very different Mannermanner, than when it first arises from some Giftgift bestowed by another, or depended on from him, as a Judgejudge loves and favoursfavors a Manman that has bribed him ;
                     •
                  or from the Relationrelation he supposes another has to him, as a Manman who loves another because he looks upon him as his Childchild. When Lovelove to another arises thus, it does truly and properly arise from Selfself-love.

There is a Sortsort of high Affectionsaffections that some have from Timetime to Timetime, that leave them without any Mannermanner of Appearanceappearance of an abiding Effecteffect. They go off suddenly; so that from the very Heightheight of their Emotionemotion, and seeming Rapturerapture, they pass at once to be quite dead, and void of all Sensesense and Activityactivity. It surely is not wont to be thus with high gracious Affectionsaffections; 
                     Do you think the holy Ghost comes on a Manman, as on Balaam, by immediate acting, and then leaves him; and then he has nothing? 
                     Shepard's Par. Part I. pP. 126. they leave a sweet Savoursavorsavour and Relishrelish of divine Thingsthings on the Heartheart, and a stronger Bentbent of Soulsoul towards God and Holinessholiness. As Moses's Faceface not only shone while he was in the Mountmount, extraordinarily conversing with God; but it continued to shine after he came down from the Mountmount. When Menmen have been conversing with Christ in an extraordinary Mannermanner, there is a sensible Effecteffect of it remains upon them; there is something remarkable in their Dispositiondisposition and Frameframe, which if we take Knowledgeknowledge of, and trace to its Causecause, we shall find it is because they have been with Jesus; Acts 4.: 13.
